\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Edge derivatives and forests on an alternating cycle}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Take the four-cycle #{0\to1\to2\to3\to0} with weights
#{(1,2,1,2)} and #{m=1}. The orthonormal vectors
##{\begin{aligned}
u_0&=\tfrac12(1,1,1,1),&u_2&=\tfrac12(1,-1,-1,1),\\
u_4&=\tfrac12(1,1,-1,-1),&u_6&=\tfrac12(1,-1,1,-1)
\end{aligned}}
have #{L_w}-eigenvalues given by their subscripts, as direct
multiplication verifies. Therefore #{Q} has eigenvalues
#{1,3,5,7}, and
##{\det Q=105,\qquad\operatorname{tr}G
 =1+\frac13+\frac15+\frac17=\frac{176}{105}.}
The edge vector #{d_{01}} has squared projection #{1} onto each
of #{u_2,u_6}, and zero onto the other two. For #{d_{12}} the
nonzero squared projections are onto #{u_4,u_6}. Half-turn
symmetry gives the other two edges. Thus
##{\frac{\partial\mathcal W}{\partial w_e}
 =\begin{cases}
 \frac12(\frac13+\frac17)=\frac5{21}&w_e=1,\\
 \frac12(\frac15+\frac17)=\frac6{35}&w_e=2.
 \end{cases}}
These are individual edge derivatives at the displayed weights;
they are not derivatives of a simultaneous change of both edges
of one weight. The weighted sum is
##{2\cdot\frac5{21}+4\cdot\frac6{35}
 =\frac{122}{105}
 =\frac12\left(4-\frac{176}{105}\right).}
This is an instance of \ref{fdip-005U}.}

\p{The forest generating polynomial is
##{\begin{aligned}
\det(tI+L_w)&=t(t+2)(t+4)(t+6)\\
 &=t^4+12t^3+44t^2+48t.
\end{aligned}}
The 4 spanning trees have weights #{4,2,4,2}, so
#{\tau_w=12} and the coefficient of #{t} is #{4\tau_w=48}.
At #{t=1}, the forest law has
##{\mathbb E[|R|]=\frac{176}{105},\qquad
\mathbb E[|F|]=\frac{244}{105}.}
Its individual edge probabilities are #{10/21} on weight-one
edges and #{24/35} on weight-two edges. They sum to
#{244/105}, as the forest identity requires.}

\p{On the unit four-cycle, all 4 edges instead have the same
effective resistance by rotation. One unit edge is in parallel
with the other three in series, giving #{R_{\mathrm{eff}}=3/4}.
Equivalently the nonconstant spectral calculation gives
#{1/2+1/4=3/4}. Foster's sum is #{4(3/4)=3}, matching
#{n-1}. This is an edge-observable limit, not a massless Gaussian
law on all 4 vertex values.}
